package cname

// Package cname holds the rules for the names a Tack program gives to C.
// It is a port of the Go compiler's package cname, whole: is_ident and
// is_keyword, Go's IsIdent and IsKeyword.
//
// An extern func's body is a C function, defined under a C name, which
// has to be one C can declare: a C identifier, ASCII letters, digits and
// underscores not starting with a digit, that is not one of C's keywords.
// The type checker holds an extern func's C name, its own or the one
// extern("name") gives, to that, and the C backend each name tack.h
// gives a type of package main or a field of a struct an extern
// signature uses; other names tack.h makes, such as lexer_Token, cannot
// be keywords.
//
// The keywords are those of C23, which include C11's and the words C23
// made keywords (bool, true, false, alignas and the rest), together with
// asm and typeof, which clang reserves in its default GNU modes. A name
// that is not a keyword but that a C header declares, such as int64_t or
// printf, is not rejected here: it clashes only with what a C file
// declares, which clang reports when it compiles that file. Go keeps the
// keywords in a map; here they are an array searched in order, since
// only the type checker and the C backend ask, once for each C name.
//
// The package is a leaf: it imports nothing of the compiler's.
